# Target C — Type-II capacity dichotomy for exact four-host return

**Date:** 2026-09-12  
**Status:** proved candidate awaiting hostile audit; theorem-state authority unchanged  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** **STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.**

The four-host regression shows that an attachment route can have zero signed-return
loss in two strict-rhombus directions when its host directions are
`{+u,-u,+v,-v}`.  This note records the source-level restriction supplied by the
Type-II near-disk geometry:

> A macroscopic attachment cell whose **only** nonzero host directions are
> `{+u,-u,+v,-v}` is an asymptotically rectangular cell centered at the force-hull
> center.  Hence there can be at most one such macroscopic cell, and its area is at
> most `D^2/2+o(D^2)`.

Consequently the Type-II area-saturation identity

\[
 \sum_r\operatorname{area}(P_{r,D})
 =\left({\pi\over4}-o(1)\right)D^2
\tag{FH.1}
\]

cannot be carried entirely by exact four-host zero-return cells.  At least one
positive-area cell must either have an additional host direction or leave the exact
four-host class.  This is a genuine capacity restriction on the regression, but it
does not yet give a positive width payment: extra hosts can still contain the same
antipodal four directions.

The candidate chain is

```text
exact four-host zero return
 -> parallelogram attachment cell
 -> near-disk annulus forces a centered rectangle
 -> at most one macroscopic cell, with area <= D^2/2+o(D^2)
 -> positive-area non-four-host cell
 -> required extra-host/interface payment or source-preserving descent.
```

The final arrow remains open.

## 1. Exact host saturation forces four side directions

For a finite host set `Q` contained in the closed unit disk, write

\[
 w_Q(x):=\max_{q\in Q}q\cdot x-\min_{q\in Q}q\cdot x
 \qquad (|x|=1).
\tag{FH.2}
\]

If `w_Q(u)=2`, then there are hosts `q_+,q_-` with

\[
q_+\cdot u=1,
\qquad q_-\cdot u=-1.
\]

Equality in Cauchy is rigid, so

\[
\boxed{w_Q(u)=2\Longrightarrow q_+=u, q_-=-u.}
\tag{FH.3}
\]

The same statement holds for a second nonparallel direction `v`.  Thus simultaneous
zero return for the two train directions of a strict rhombus,

\[
2-w_Q(u)=2-w_Q(v)=0,
\tag{FH.4}
\]

requires the four antipodal host directions

\[
\{+u,-u,+v,-v\}\subset Q.
\tag{FH.5}
\]

For a harmonic attachment cell `P=conv(A)`, the nonzero hosts are the normalized
displacements of consecutive attachment vertices.  If these are the **only** four
directions in (FH.5), every side of `P` is parallel to `u` or `v`.  After suppressing
collinear redundant vertices, a convex polygon with these four cyclic side directions
has exactly one side in each direction and is a parallelogram.

This is the precise scope of “exact four-host cell” below.  It does not include a cell
whose host set contains additional directions, even if those extra hosts do not change
the width in (FH.4).

## 2. Approximate cyclic parallelograms are rectangles

Let `P_D` be a sequence of such parallelograms in the Type-II branch.  Translate the
Type-II force-hull center to the origin and put `R_D=D/2`.  Canonical near-disk
stability gives a number `epsilon_D->0` such that every force-hull vertex, and hence
every attachment vertex, satisfies

\[
 R_D-\epsilon_DD\le |x|\le R_D+\epsilon_DD.
\tag{FH.6}
\]

Write the four vertices of `P_D` as

\[
 c_D\pm r_D\pm s_D.
\tag{FH.7}
\]

Assume for the moment that

\[
\operatorname{area}(P_D)\ge\eta D^2
\tag{FH.8}
\]

for a fixed `eta>0`.  Squaring (FH.6) at the four sign choices and subtracting
opposite and adjacent values gives

\[
 c_D\cdot r_D=o(D^2),
\qquad
 c_D\cdot s_D=o(D^2),
\qquad
 r_D\cdot s_D=o(D^2).
\tag{FH.9}
\]

Indeed each difference of squared norms is `O(epsilon_DD^2)`.  The area lower bound
and the `O(D)` diameter bound on `r_D,s_D` make `r_D,s_D` uniformly nondegenerate in
the two-dimensional sense; solving the two scalar equations for `c_D` therefore gives

\[
\boxed{|c_D|=o(D).}
\tag{FH.10}
\]

Average the four squared norms in (FH.6).  Using (FH.9)--(FH.10),

\[
 |r_D|^2+|s_D|^2=R_D^2+o(D^2).
\tag{FH.11}
\]

The parallelogram area is

\[
\operatorname{area}(P_D)=4|r_D\times s_D|
 \le 2(|r_D|^2+|s_D|^2)
 ={D^2\over2}+o(D^2),
\tag{FH.12}
\]

where the inequality is `2|r\times s|<=|r|^2+|s|^2`.

Moreover (FH.8) and the `O(D)` bounds imply that the distances from `c_D` to the
four side lines are bounded below by a positive multiple of `D`.  Together with
(FH.10), this gives

\[
\boxed{0\in\operatorname{int}(P_D)\quad\text{for all sufficiently large }D.}
\tag{FH.13}
\]

So a macroscopic exact four-host cell is a centered, asymptotically rectangular
carrier.  The area bound (FH.12) is sharp at the square limit.

## 3. At most one macroscopic exact four-host cell

Canonical harmonic-cell disjointness gives pairwise disjoint cell interiors.  If two
cells each satisfy (FH.8) and the exact four-host hypothesis, (FH.13) puts the origin
in the interior of both.  Their interiors would then overlap, which is impossible.

Hence for every fixed `eta>0` there is at most one exact four-host cell with area at
least `eta D^2`.

The canonical square-root budget

\[
\sum_r\sqrt{\operatorname{area}(P_{r,D})}=O(D)
\tag{FH.14}
\]

controls the remaining cells.  If `area(P_{r,D})<eta D^2`, then

\[
\sum_{\operatorname{area}(P_{r,D})<\eta D^2}
\operatorname{area}(P_{r,D})
\le
\sqrt\eta D\sum_r\sqrt{\operatorname{area}(P_{r,D})}
=O(\sqrt\eta)D^2.
\tag{FH.15}
\]

Suppose every cell of area at least `eta D^2` were exact four-host.  By the preceding
paragraph and (FH.12), the total cell area would satisfy

\[
\sum_r\operatorname{area}(P_{r,D})
\le {D^2\over2}+O(\sqrt\eta)D^2+o(D^2).
\tag{FH.16}
\]

Choose `eta` small enough that the `O(\sqrt\eta)` coefficient is less than
`(\pi-2)/4`.  This contradicts (FH.1).  Therefore:

\[
\boxed{
\text{some cell of area }\ge\eta D^2\text{ has an additional host direction
or is outside the exact four-host class.}}
\tag{FH.17}
\]

The conclusion is a fixed positive-area alternative, not merely a statement about a
single vanishing cell.

## 4. Relation to the live C-II currency

For a cell with attachment-route host set `Q_{att}`, the audited route theorem gives

\[
d_rE_{att,r}\ge
\sum_{R\subset P_r}\operatorname{area}(R)
\bigl[4-w_{Q_{att}}(u_R)-w_{Q_{att}}(v_R)\bigr].
\tag{FH.18}
\]

The exact four-host regression makes the bracket vanish for a two-direction square
grid.  The dichotomy above shows that a Type-II near-disk cell system cannot put all
of its leading area into that exact regression class.  It does **not** show that the
bracket in (FH.18) is positive on the remaining cell: additional hosts may still
contain the same antipodal four directions.  A closing step must therefore prove one
of the following source-level statements:

1. additional host directions force a positive-width train population;
2. the interfaces needed to carry the remaining `\pi/4-1/2` area pay the aggregate
   outer-detour currency; or
3. the centered rectangle branch admits a simultaneous source-preserving shear or an
   exact unit equilateral triangle.

None of these three statements is proved here.  The theorem is a capacity restriction
on the four-host regression, classified as a proved candidate awaiting hostile audit;
the canonical theorem state remains unchanged and C-II remains open.


